Edition No. 23 — Thursday, 22 October 2026

10 stories: Twelve months to the day: Raphael Nakacinda walks out of Chimbokaila after serving his defamation sentence, with a sedition count still to be ruled on in November (lead); A US$1.6 billion second act in Chilanga: Hichilema breaks ground on the UCF urea expansion and warns officials who slow projects that he will be ‘ruthless’; ‘Not the rainfall of 1964’: a regulator tells the Insaka that Zambia’s tailings dams are not ready for three million tonnes of copper a year; Posters, not practitioners: Lusaka’s mayor says his crackdown on illegal adverts is not aimed at traditional healers, after their association asks him to tread carefully; 859 dead in three months: police count 12,865 road crashes between July and September, up 27 per cent on last year, as a head-on collision near Zimba kills two Americans; Drilling for a virus on the doorstep: Luapula tests its Ebola response with mobile laboratories as Nigeria becomes the fifth country with a case from the DRC outbreak; Two Public Procurement Act convictions in a week: Chongwe’s district health director fined K330,000 over contracts to his own company; a former Solwezi council officer jailed; Ahead twice, beaten in the 100th minute: the Copper Princesses lose 3–2 to Venezuela and go into Saturday’s match with France without a point; Thirty-two hours, 214 kilometres and the last lap alone: Cosmas Bwalya sets a Zambian record at the Backyard Ultra World Team Championship at York Farm; Forty years of NATAAZ: the national theatre festival gathers in Chingola, with a K100,000 grant per arts association and a nudge towards the digital stage.


